/* Pass 23B: canonical chapter parity for Fallout and Harry Potter. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-09":{"number":9,"title":"Power Armor","content":"Power armor training began because I made one arrogant remark.\n\nThis was my fault.\n\nDanse had been repairing a Brotherhood frame in the garage when I said, “It can’t be that difficult. You climb in and walk.”\n\nHe slowly lowered the wrench.\n\nHaylen, sitting three feet away, looked delighted.\n\nRhys whispered, “Oh, no.”\n\nDanse turned toward me.\n\n“Climb in.”\n\nI should have recognized the trap.\n\nInstead I said, “Gladly.”\n\nTen minutes later I was trapped inside several hundred pounds of technology trying not to walk through a wall.\n\n“Small movements,” Danse called from outside.\n\n“I am making small movements.”\n\n“You just kicked a toolbox across the garage.”\n\n“It was in my operational area.”\n\n“It was six feet away.”\n\n“Was.”\n\nHaylen covered her mouth.\n\n\"Don't laugh,\" I said from inside the frame.\n\n\"I'm not.\" Haylen's shoulders made a convincing argument against her.\n\nDanse pointed at the orange lever beside my right hip. \"Emergency release. Tell me what it does.\"\n\n\"Gets me out when I've embarrassed myself enough?\"\n\n\"It disengages the rear locking assembly.\" His expression didn't change. \"If the hydraulics lose power, you need to know how to exit without waiting for an external operator. Show me.\"\n\nI reached for it. Nothing happened.\n\n\"Wrong lever,\" Haylen said, suddenly serious. She came over and held her finger beside a faded caution stripe. \"This is the one you can reach while the frame is powered down. The orange one only releases the exterior latch.\"\n\nDanse leaned in to inspect the marking. Somebody had applied a fresh coat of paint over most of the old label.\n\n\"Who signed off on that?\" he asked.\n\nRhys stopped smiling. \"I did the exterior, sir. Wasn't told the safety label was underneath.\"\n\n\"Then we correct it. And check the others before anyone else trains.\"\n\nHaylen fetched a marker and documented the latch position while Danse walked me through the actual release. I wasn't allowed to repeat the drill until I could find it without looking.\n\nWhen I finally climbed free, my knees felt like jelly. Danse offered a hand; I took it, braced for another lecture, and got something more useful.\n\n\"Good correction,\" he said. \"You asked when the instructions didn't match the machine.\"\n\n\"I didn't ask. I complained.\"\n\n\"In your case the distinction is not always apparent.\" His mouth moved a fraction. I laughed so hard I had to sit on the garage step.\n\nDanse did not laugh.\n\nThat would have been less humiliating.\n\nHe watched me with the solemn focus of a man observing a controlled demolition.\n\n“Again. Shift your weight before the step.”\n\n“I know how walking works.”\n\n“Not in armor, apparently.”\n\nI turned too quickly.\n\nThe frame lurched.\n\nDanse caught the chest plate with both hands.\n\n“Stop.”\n\nI froze.\n\nHe was close enough that I could see his face through the open frame.\n\n“Don’t fight the servos,” he said. “The suit amplifies what you give it. If you panic and overcorrect, it overcorrects with you.”\n\n“Easy for you to say.”\n\n“I fell the first time.”\n\nI stared.\n\nHe stared back.\n\n“Paladin Danse fell over in power armor?”\n\n“Once.”\n\n“Tell me everything.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did anyone see?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Was Cutler there?”\n\nHis expression changed.\n\nFor a second I regretted asking.\n\nThen he exhaled through his nose.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And?”\n\n“He never stopped mentioning it.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Then I respect him more already.”\n\nDanse’s eyes softened.\n\n“He would have liked you.”\n\nThe words slipped out.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nI felt the power armor’s motors humming around me.\n\n“Yeah?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis hands were still braced against the frame near my ribs.\n\nNot touching me.\n\nClose enough that my body noticed anyway.\n\nDanse seemed to realize the same thing.\n\nHe stepped back.\n\n“Try again.”\n\nHis voice was rougher.\n\nI obeyed.\n\nOnce I stopped trying to control the suit like a stubborn brahmin, it became easier. The weight shifted where I wanted. Hydraulic strength turned steps into certainty. The world felt smaller from inside all that metal.\n\n“I understand the appeal,” I admitted.\n\nDanse folded his arms.\n\n“Of course you do.”\n\n“It’s like being a building that can punch people.”\n\n“That is an appalling description.”\n\n“Accurate, though.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Can I keep it?”\n\n“Absolutely not.”\n\nBy midday he had me walking, turning, crouching, and stepping over obstacles without destroying station property.\n\nThe final lesson was emergency exit.\n\nDanse opened the frame and showed me how to release the locks manually.\n\n“If the power core fails, don’t stay inside.”\n\n“Seems obvious.”\n\n“It isn’t when people think the armor is safer than whatever’s outside.”\n\nHis hands moved over the latches with practiced speed.\n\n“Armor is equipment. Not security.”\n\nSomething about the sentence caught me.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nDanse was focused on the mechanism.\n\n“You believe that?”\n\n“What?”\n\n“That armor isn’t security.”\n\nHe glanced up.\n\n“It’s steel.”\n\n“You wear it whenever possible.”\n\n“It’s practical.”\n\n“You also wear the invisible kind.”\n\nThe garage went quiet.\n\nHaylen had wisely disappeared sometime in the last hour.\n\nDanse’s expression closed.\n\n“I don’t know what you mean.”\n\n“Yes, you do.”\n\nHe stepped away from the frame.\n\n“This isn’t the time.”\n\n“Fine.”\n\nI did not push.\n\nThat was something I was learning with him too: pressure was not the same as persistence. Some doors opened if I waited near them without pretending they were not doors.\n\nI climbed out of the armor.\n\nMy knees felt strangely light after carrying the suit’s weight.\n\nDanse handed me a bottle of water.\n\n“Hydrate.”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“I’m serious.”\n\n“So am I.”\n\nHe frowned while I drank.\n\nA drop ran down my chin. I wiped it away.\n\nHis eyes followed the movement.\n\nThen snapped back to my face.\n\nInteresting.\n\nI lowered the bottle.\n\nDanse suddenly found the armor frame extremely compelling.\n\n“Your footwork improved.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You still turn too sharply.”\n\n“There’s the corrective feedback.”\n\n“It’s necessary.”\n\n“I was worried you were ill.”\n\nHe reached for the wrench.\n\nI caught his wrist.\n\nThe gesture was impulsive.\n\nDanse stopped.\n\nHis skin was warm.\n\nSo was mine, abruptly.\n\nI released him.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe looked at the place where my hand had been.\n\n“It’s fine.”\n\nThe silence stretched.\n\nOutside, a vertibird did not appear. No alarm sounded. Rhys did not walk in to rescue either of us from ourselves.\n\nDanse cleared his throat.\n\n“You did well today.”\n\n“Just well?”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\n“Outstanding.”\n\nThere was no sarcasm in it.\n\nI smiled before I could stop myself.\n\nHe looked away first.\n\nThat evening, I found a set of handwritten power armor maintenance notes beside my bedroll.\n\nThe handwriting was blocky, precise, and unmistakably Danse’s.\n\nAt the bottom he had added one line:\n\nDO NOT KICK TOOLBOXES.\n\nI laughed loudly enough to wake Rhys.\n\nFrom the other side of the station, Danse pretended not to hear.\n\nThe training did not end with walking.\n\nDanse believed that if I was going to use Brotherhood equipment even temporarily, I was going to learn every way it could kill me before an enemy got the chance.\n\n“Fusion core failure.”\n\n“Exit the frame.”\n\n“Hydraulic lock in the right leg.”\n\n“Shift weight, disengage assist, call you something unprintable.”\n\n“The third step is not part of procedure.”\n\n“It improves morale.”\n\nHe made me repeat emergency egress until I could do it with my eyes closed.\n\nLiterally.\n\nI stood inside the frame with the helmet display dark while Danse circled me.\n\n“Left release.”\n\nI found it by touch.\n\n“Manual torso latch.”\n\nFound that too.\n\n“Rear access.”\n\nI reached back, caught the handle, and pulled.\n\nNothing happened.\n\nI pulled harder.\n\nStill nothing.\n\nDanse swore.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Don’t move.”\n\n“That phrase is never comforting.”\n\nHe stepped behind me and worked at the jammed latch. His chest brushed the rear armor plate as he leaned around the frame. One hand closed over mine to reposition my grip.\n\n“Here.”\n\nHis voice was suddenly much closer without a helmet between us.\n\nI became aware of several completely irrelevant facts at once: the heat of his hand, the rasp of stubble when he spoke near my ear, the way he smelled like gun oil and clean sweat.\n\nThe latch released with a metallic crack.\n\nI nearly fell backward into him.\n\nDanse caught my waist.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\n“Oh,” I said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis hands remained exactly where they had landed.\n\nI turned my head.\n\nHe was looking at me with the expression of a man who had just discovered an unexploded mine under his own boot.\n\n“You can let go.”\n\n“I’m aware.”\n\nHe did not.\n\nA second passed.\n\nThen another.\n\nFrom the station doorway Haylen yelled, “Paladin, Rhys wants to know if you’re planning to train the recruit or marry the power armor.”\n\nDanse released me so fast I almost fell for real.\n\n“I am not a recruit!” I shouted back.\n\n“Noted!”\n\nDanse walked three full steps away and stared at the wall.\n\nI climbed out of the frame.\n\n“Are you all right?” I asked with criminal innocence.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You look warm.”\n\n“It’s the ambient temperature.”\n\n“It’s forty-eight degrees.”\n\n“The training room retains heat.”\n\n“There’s a hole in the roof.”\n\nHe turned.\n\n“Do you want to complete the lesson or not?”\n\nI bit the inside of my cheek.\n\n“Yes, Paladin.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n“Again.”\n\nWe practiced until my muscles shook.\n\nWhen I finally climbed out for the last time, he handed me water and leaned against the armor rack beside me.\n\n“You did well.”\n\n“Even with the toolbox?”\n\n“Especially after the toolbox. You corrected the mistake.”\n\nI looked at him over the bottle.\n\n“You always do that.”\n\n“Do what?”\n\n“Treat mistakes like they’re only useful if you learn from them.”\n\n“That’s generally true.”\n\n“Even yours?”\n\nHis face changed.\n\nThe question had gone somewhere I had not meant it to.\n\n“Yes,” he said after a moment. “Especially mine.”\n\nI thought of four names on a wall.\n\nDanse looked away.\n\nThen, quieter, “Though some lessons cost more than they should.”\n\nThe frame hissed softly behind me as its servos cooled.\n\nDanse picked up the toolbox I had kicked earlier and began returning everything to its assigned slot. I watched him line up three wrenches by size.\n\n“You know,” I said, “normal people would leave that until later.”\n\n“Normal people lose tools.”\n\n“Normal people also occasionally enjoy themselves.”\n\nHe slid the smallest wrench into place. “Training can be enjoyable.”\n\n“You enjoyed watching me nearly walk through a wall.”\n\nHis mouth betrayed him.\n\n“I did not.”\n\n“You absolutely did.”\n\n“Your recovery was impressive.”\n\n“That is a confession with extra steps.”\n\nDanse shook his head, but the tension around his eyes eased. The lesson about mistakes was not abstract for him. He treated every error like something that had to justify its cost. Maybe that was why watching me make a harmless one unsettled him and amused him at the same time.\n\nSome mistakes only cost a toolbox.\n\nI wanted him to learn that too.\n\nI did not reach for him.\n\nNot yet.\n\nInstead I set the water down and climbed back into the frame.\n\n“Again?”\n\nHis eyes returned to me.\n\nThis time the smile came openly.\n\n“Again.”\n","word_count":1836},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-24":{"number":24,"title":"The List","content":"Danse knew something was wrong before anyone told him.\n\nThat was the cruelty of being observant.\n\nQuinlan stopped meeting his eyes.\n\nKells requested reports he had already received.\n\nMaxson cancelled a briefing without explanation.\n\nHaylen found reasons to be elsewhere whenever Danse entered the room.\n\nHe noticed every piece.\n\nFor two days he said nothing.\n\nI found Haylen in the radio room late that second night. She had dismantled a receiver that was working perfectly well, which told me she needed a task more than the machine needed fixing.\n\n\"Did you tell him?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you going to?\"\n\nShe held up a wire with a frayed end. \"This is the one thing in the room I can actually repair.\"\n\nThe statement did not sound like Haylen. She was usually the one person who would turn a messy problem into a checklist before Rhys finished complaining about it.\n\n\"What do you know?\" I asked.\n\nShe looked toward the open hatch. Voices carried from the crew deck, too close for privacy. She shut the door, then stepped away from it.\n\n\"I've seen people making copies of records they don't need for routine supply work,\" she said. \"I know which office requests them. I don't know why they want them, and I'm not going to pretend I have proof of an order I haven't seen.\"\n\n\"Could you be mistaken?\"\n\n\"Of course I could.\" Her face crumpled a little on the word. \"That's what I keep hoping.\"\n\nShe gave me the date of one request and asked me not to take her place in the chain of custody. The receiver still lay open on the bench. She hadn't told me anything that would let me save Danse with a speech or accuse the wrong person with certainty.\n\nWhen I left, Haylen picked up the frayed wire again. I wanted to promise her it would be all right. I hadn't earned that promise. Instead I asked whether she wanted company while she worked. She nodded without looking up.\n\nOn the third night he found me near the Prydwen’s aft storage section.\n\n“Walk with me.”\n\nNot a request.\n\nI followed.\n\nHe led me to an empty maintenance corridor and stopped.\n\n“Has anyone spoken to you about me?”\n\nMy stomach tightened.\n\n“No.”\n\n“That was too fast.”\n\n“It was true.”\n\nDanse studied my face.\n\n“Something’s happening.”\n\n“I noticed.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\nHe turned away.\n\n“Quinlan’s Institute data.”\n\n“What about it?”\n\n“He decoded part of an escapee registry.”\n\nThe words sounded ordinary.\n\nHis expression did not.\n\n“I asked to see it. He refused.”\n\n“That doesn’t mean it’s about you.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“But you think it is.”\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n“I think people I trust are behaving like they know something I don’t.”\n\nFear looked strange on him.\n\nNot because he was never afraid.\n\nBecause he usually converted fear into action before anyone could see it.\n\nNow there was no target.\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\nHe let me take it.\n\n“Whatever it is, we handle it.”\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe looked down at our fingers.\n\n“You say that a lot.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“That you don’t know.”\n\n“Reality refuses to brief me in advance.”\n\nHis mouth moved faintly.\n\nThen boots sounded at the end of the corridor.\n\nHaylen appeared.\n\nShe stopped when she saw us holding hands.\n\nUsually she would have smiled.\n\nThis time she looked like she might cry.\n\nDanse released me.\n\n“Scribe.”\n\n“Paladin.”\n\nHe stared at her.\n\n“Tell me.”\n\nHaylen’s face went pale.\n\n“I can’t.”\n\n“That confirms there’s something to tell.”\n\n“Danse—”\n\n“Is my command in question?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“My loyalty?”\n\nShe looked away.\n\nDanse flinched as if struck.\n\n“Haylen.”\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\nShe left before he could stop her.\n\nDanse stood motionless.\n\nI touched his arm.\n\nHe pulled away.\n\nNot from me.\n\nFrom everything.\n\n“I need to speak with Quinlan.”\n\n“Let me come.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“This is Brotherhood business.”\n\n“So am I, apparently.”\n\nHis expression hardened.\n\n“Not this.”\n\nThen he walked away.\n\nHe did not return that night.\n\nOr the next morning.\n\nBy afternoon, his bunk was untouched.\n\nHis power armor station was empty.\n\nThe Prydwen felt wrong without him.\n\nNobody would answer me.\n\nRhys looked genuinely frightened when I asked.\n\nKells ordered me to stand down.\n\nQuinlan would only say, “The matter is under review.”\n\nI considered throwing him over the rail.\n\nHaylen found me first.\n\nShe pulled me into the same maintenance corridor where Danse had questioned me.\n\nHer hands were shaking.\n\n“He’s gone.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“No. I mean gone. He left the Prydwen.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“You know something else.”\n\nHaylen closed her eyes.\n\n“The Institute list included a synth designation. M7-97.”\n\nMy blood went cold.\n\nShe continued because stopping would have been worse.\n\n“The DNA profile matched Danse.”\n\nFor a moment the airship vanished.\n\nThere was only the sound of Danse saying I’m not a machine on the road outside ArcJet.\n\nNick Valentine beneath Diamond City lights.\n\nAmos in his greenhouse.\n\nCutler changing beneath his skin.\n\nDanse’s absolute belief that categories were truth.\n\n“No,” I said.\n\nHaylen’s face broke.\n\n“I checked it three times.”\n\n“That doesn’t mean—”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Does Maxson know?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What’s the order?”\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nI knew before she spoke.\n\n“Execution.”\n\nThe word turned the corridor into ice.\n\nI pushed past her.\n\nHaylen caught my arm.\n\n“Wait.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Maxson is looking for you.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“No. Listen to me.”\n\nHer grip tightened.\n\n“Danse has a fallback location. Listening Post Bravo. He used it in case Cambridge was compromised.”\n\nI stared at her.\n\n“You’re telling me where he is.”\n\n“I’m telling you where he might be.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nTears stood in her eyes.\n\n“Because he saved my life more times than I can count.”\n\nI thought of Danse calling attachment dangerous.\n\nToo late.\n\nIt had already happened to all of us.\n\nI squeezed Haylen’s hand once.\n\nThen I went to find Maxson.\n\nMaxson was not in his command quarters when I found him.\n\nKells was.\n\nThe captain took one look at my face and shut the door behind me.\n\n“You have been ordered to stand by.”\n\n“Where is Danse?”\n\n“That information is restricted.”\n\n“So you know.”\n\nKells’ expression did not change.\n\n“Paladin Danse is the subject of a security investigation.”\n\n“His name is on an Institute escape list.”\n\nThat made him pause.\n\nHaylen’s disclosure had crossed a line. I knew it. So did he.\n\nKells folded his hands behind his back.\n\n“You should leave this alone.”\n\n“Can you?”\n\nHis eyes sharpened.\n\n“This is not about me.”\n\n“It’s about a man you served with.”\n\n“It is about a synth infiltrating the Brotherhood.”\n\nThe phrasing was already changing him.\n\nNot Danse.\n\nA synth.\n\nAn infiltrator.\n\nI felt sick.\n\n“Did Quinlan find evidence he was sending information to the Institute?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Sabotage?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Hidden orders?”\n\n“Unknown.”\n\n“So the only thing that changed is what you learned he is.”\n\nKells’ mouth tightened.\n\n“That alone is sufficient.”\n\nFor the Brotherhood, maybe it was.\n\nThat was the horror.\n\nI left before the argument became useless.\n\nHaylen met me near the lower gantry. Rhys stood with her.\n\nThat surprised me more than anything that day.\n\nHe looked furious.\n\nNot at me.\n\nAt the deck.\n\n“Rhys?”\n\n“Don’t.”\n\n“I didn’t say anything.”\n\n“You were going to.”\n\nHaylen touched his arm.\n\nRhys pulled away and paced two steps.\n\n“He’s an asshole,” he said.\n\nI stared.\n\n“Danse?”\n\n“Maxson.”\n\nHaylen closed her eyes.\n\nRhys kept going.\n\n“Danse dragged me out of Cambridge after Keane went down. Carried me in power armor for three miles because I couldn’t put weight on my leg.”\n\nHis voice became rough.\n\n“He sat with Worwick when he was dying. He wrote every family notification himself even though half of us didn’t have families left to notify.”\n\nRhys swallowed hard.\n\n“And now I’m supposed to believe none of that counts because some Institute file has a number on it?”\n\nI had never liked him more.\n\n“Do you?” I asked.\n\nHe looked at me as if I had insulted him.\n\n“I don’t know what I believe.”\n\nHonest.\n\nPainfully so.\n\nHaylen rubbed at her eyes.\n\n“We can’t all leave. If we do, they’ll know.”\n\n“Know what?” Rhys asked.\n\n“That we’re helping.”\n\nHe looked toward the command deck.\n\nThen at me.\n\n“What do you need?”\n\nThe question almost undid me.\n\n“Time.”\n\nRhys nodded.\n\n“I can create a maintenance fault in the flight schedule.”\n\nHaylen stared at him.\n\n“You can?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n“But Lancer-Captain Kells thinks I’m an idiot, so if I break something by accident nobody will be surprised.”\n\nDespite everything, I laughed.\n\nHaylen did too, once, wetly.\n\nFor one moment we were back in Cambridge and Danse was about to tell all of us to act professionally.\n\nThe absence of his voice hurt.\n\nRhys’ expression changed.\n\n“Find him.”\n\n“I will.”\n\n“And if he…”\n\nHe could not finish.\n\nI understood.\n\n“If I can bring him back, I will.”\n\nNot to the Brotherhood.\n\nTo himself.\n\nRhys nodded.\n\nHaylen pressed the access code for Bravo into my palm.\n\nRhys swore under his breath.\n\nHaylen immediately started checking the corridor as if Maxson might personally emerge from a maintenance hatch.\n\n“Listen,” she said. “Danse won’t run because he thinks he deserves whatever happens.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“He’ll call it duty.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“He’ll make it sound reasonable.”\n\nThat frightened me because she was right.\n\nRhys leaned against the bulkhead, arms crossed so tightly his knuckles had gone pale.\n\n“For what it’s worth,” he said, “I thought he was an asshole when I met him.”\n\nHaylen stared at him.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You still think he’s an asshole.”\n\n“Yeah, but he’s *our* asshole.”\n\nThe laugh escaped me before I could stop it.\n\nHaylen covered her mouth.\n\nRhys looked offended. “I’m trying to be supportive.”\n\n“You’re doing great.”\n\nHis expression crumpled for half a second.\n\n“Just bring him back.”\n\nThere were no jokes left after that.\n\nHaylen pulled me into a brief, fierce hug before I could react.\n\nShe stepped back immediately, wiping at her face with the heel of her hand.\n\n“If he says he deserves it, don’t believe him.”\n\n“I won’t.”\n\n“He’ll sound convincing.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nRhys looked toward the ceiling speakers. “Maxson’s going to notice if you don’t go.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nNobody moved.\n\nThe Prydwen hummed around us, enormous and familiar to them, suddenly hostile in a way the metal had not changed enough to explain.\n\nThe ship was the same.\n\nThe category attached to Danse was different.\n\nApparently that was all it took.\n\nThen the shipwide speakers called my designation to Maxson’s command deck.\n\nThe three of us looked at one another.\n\n“Guess he’s ready,” I said.\n\nHaylen gripped my hand.\n\n“Don’t let him make you forget who Danse is.”\n\nI closed my fingers around the code.\n\n“That’s the one thing I’m sure of.”\n","word_count":1784},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-39":{"number":39,"title":"To Victory","content":"Danse stopped saying Ad Victoriam for almost four months.\n\nI noticed because he used to say it constantly.\n\nBefore missions. After briefings. As greeting, farewell, encouragement, punctuation.\n\nAfter banishment, the phrase vanished.\n\nI never asked why.\n\nThen one morning, settlers arrived at our house asking for help.\n\nA group of raiders had taken the bridge north of their farms. Not a Brotherhood concern. Not strategic technology. No elder had issued an order.\n\nJust people in danger.\n\nDanse picked up his rifle.\n\n“Let’s go.”\n\nThe fight was difficult.\n\nThe raiders had fortified the bridge with mines and firing positions. Danse studied the approach, planned routes, assigned civilians who insisted on helping to safe positions, and then looked at me.\n\n“Ready?”\n\n“Always.”\n\n“That’s objectively false.”\n\n“Romantic.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe moved.\n\nThe battle felt different from Brotherhood operations only because nobody above us would write a report.\n\nDanse was still Danse.\n\nHe called targets. Protected flanks. Shouted at one settler for exposing himself. Took a round against his armor and kept moving.\n\nI was still me.\n\nI argued with a tactical instruction in the middle of gunfire.\n\nHe yelled that we would discuss it later.\n\nWe won.\n\nNot cleanly. One settler took a bullet through the leg. A section of bridge collapsed. My coat caught fire briefly, which Danse found much less funny than I did.\n\nBut the raiders fled.\n\nThe road reopened.\n\nAs the settlers carried the wounded home, Danse stood on the bridge watching them go.\n\nI moved beside him.\n\n“Objective complete?”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Successful thing?”\n\nHis smile appeared.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe wind moved over the river.\n\nDanse stared toward the settlement.\n\n“Ad Victoriam,” he said.\n\nI turned.\n\nHe seemed surprised by the words too.\n\n“I thought you were done with that.”\n\n“So did I.”\n\n“What changed?”\n\nDanse considered.\n\n“I used to think victory meant the Brotherhood’s objective survived.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“The people did.”\n\nSimple.\n\nEnough.\n\nI took his hand.\n\n“To victory,” I said.\n\nHis fingers closed around mine.\n\nWe walked home beneath a clear sky.\n\nThat night we ate too much, drank beer on the porch, and listened to Danse play six entire chords badly enough that the dog from the neighboring farm left.\n\n“You’re improving,” I lied.\n\n“My transitions are cleaner.”\n\n“The dog disagrees.”\n\n“The dog has no musical training.”\n\n“Neither do you.”\n\nDanse set the guitar aside.\n\n“Come here.”\n\n“That sounded like an order.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“I thought we established—”\n\nHe pulled me into his lap and kissed me.\n\nI forgot the argument.\n\nLater, the lantern burned low in the bedroom.\n\nDanse sat beside me with the confidence that had grown slowly between us, always pausing long enough to make choice visible. I answered by leaning into him. We kissed until the argument had become a joke again, then let the kiss end without forcing it to become anything else.\n\nWe lay tangled beneath the blankets while rain began outside.\n\nDanse traced one finger along my hand.\n\n“Do you regret it?” he asked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Choosing me.”\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Even knowing what I am?”\n\n“Especially knowing who you are.”\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\nThe difference between what and who had once seemed impossible to him.\n\nNow he breathed through it.\n\n“Ad Victoriam,” I whispered.\n\nDanse smiled in the dark.\n\n“Ad Victoriam.”\n\nThe bridge fight brought visitors for days.\n\nSettlers arrived with food, ammunition, spare lumber, and gratitude Danse did not know what to do with.\n\nHe tried to refuse everything.\n\nMara ignored him and put two jars of preserved vegetables on our kitchen shelf.\n\nThe workshop owner left a replacement set of guitar strings.\n\nA farmer delivered a bag of coffee beans and said, “Maybe whoever makes yours can save you.”\n\nI nearly collapsed laughing.\n\nDanse stood in the doorway holding the beans like evidence in a murder case.\n\n“My coffee is not that bad.”\n\nThe farmer had already left.\n\n“It has become a regional issue,” I told him.\n\n“This is slander.”\n\n“Public health concern.”\n\nHe pointed the bag at me.\n\n“You encouraged this.”\n\n“Ad Victoriam.”\n\nThe expression that crossed his face stopped me.\n\nNot pain exactly.\n\nSurprise at hearing the words in our kitchen.\n\nI set the joke aside.\n\n“Too much?”\n\n“No.”\n\nDanse placed the beans on the counter.\n\n“I’m trying to understand why it felt different on the bridge.”\n\n“You told me. The people survived.”\n\n“That’s part of it.”\n\nHe leaned against the counter.\n\n“When I said it before, victory was always attached to a larger campaign. Recover the technology. destroy the target. secure the area. advance the Brotherhood.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“Now I think victory can be smaller.”\n\nHe looked around the house.\n\n“A repaired pump. A road people can use. Lena staying where she chose. Three chords.”\n\n“Four.”\n\n“Four.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Coffee not killing anyone.”\n\n“That has never occurred.”\n\n“Debatable.”\n\nHe came closer.\n\n“Do you know why I kept the phrase?”\n\n“You like Latin?”\n\n“I don’t speak Latin.”\n\n“That has never stopped anybody.”\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n“Because it reminded me that survival was supposed to lead somewhere.”\n\nThe humor left me.\n\nDanse rested his hands on my hips.\n\n“For a while after Bravo, I thought surviving Maxson’s order was the end of something. Maybe it was.”\n\nHe looked toward the workshop window.\n\n“But I did not know there could be another direction afterward.”\n\n“To whatever comes next.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\n“That.”\n\nA knock interrupted us.\n\nRhys stood on the porch.\n\nFor one terrifying second neither Danse nor I moved.\n\nRhys wore civilian clothes beneath a Brotherhood jacket with the insignia covered by a strip of cloth. No power armor. No escort.\n\nDanse’s face went empty.\n\n“Knight Rhys.”\n\nRhys winced.\n\n“Don’t.”\n\nDanse’s hands dropped from me.\n\n“What are you doing here?”\n\n“Haylen sent this.”\n\nHe held out a sealed packet.\n\nDanse did not take it.\n\nRhys looked at me.\n\nI stayed out of reach of the decision.\n\nFinally Danse accepted the packet.\n\n“Is she safe?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Are you?”\n\nRhys shrugged.\n\n“Depends who you ask.”\n\nThat was so Rhys I nearly laughed.\n\nDanse’s mouth moved too.\n\nNeither man seemed to know what to do next.\n\nSo Rhys did what soldiers do when emotion becomes unbearable.\n\nHe inspected the porch railing.\n\n“This brace is wrong.”\n\nDanse’s head snapped toward it.\n\n“It is not.”\n\n“Angle’s off.”\n\n“It has held through three storms.”\n\n“Still wrong.”\n\nFive minutes later they were arguing over carpentry like nothing had happened.\n\nIt was not nothing.\n\nThat was why it worked.\n\nRhys stayed for dinner.\n\nHe did not apologize for the Brotherhood. Danse did not ask him to. They talked about Haylen, the Gladius dead, the Prydwen, new recruits, a scribe who had accidentally dropped a wrench through a maintenance grate and shut down half a deck.\n\nDanse listened like a starving man trying not to eat too fast.\n\nWhen Rhys left, he stood at the bottom of the porch steps.\n\n“I don’t know what I’m supposed to call you.”\n\nDanse looked at him.\n\n“Danse.”\n\nRhys nodded.\n\n“Right.”\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n“Ad Victoriam.”\n\nDanse’s eyes closed for half a second.\n\nThen he answered.\n\n“Ad Victoriam.”\n\nRhys walked away.\n\nDanse remained on the porch holding Haylen’s packet.\n\nInside was a letter, four pages in her cramped handwriting, and Keane’s old baseball card from the casualty wall in Cambridge.\n\nDanse sat down hard.\n\nI sat beside him.\n\nHe read the letter twice.\n\nThen pressed the card against his mouth and cried quietly.\n\nNot from despair.\n\nNot entirely from grief.\n\nFrom connection surviving a border he thought had become absolute.\n\nWhen he could speak, he looked at me.\n\n“She doesn’t regret knowing me.”\n\nI remembered the unsent letter at Bravo.\n\n“No.”\n\n“She said she’s furious.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Of course she did.”\n\nDanse laughed through tears.\n\nHaylen’s letter contained no grand declaration.\n\nThat made it more painful.\n\nShe complained about Rhys. Described a new scribe who mislabeled half the station’s medical supplies. Told Danse the old transmitter still cut out whenever it rained. Wrote that Maxson had not rescinded the order barring him from Brotherhood facilities and that she was not stupid enough to test it.\n\nThen, near the end, one line made Danse stop breathing for a second.\n\n*Cambridge still feels like your station sometimes.*\n\nHe handed me the page because his hands had started shaking.\n\nI read it twice.\n\n“She misses you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“She’s angry.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“She sent you Keane’s card.”\n\nDanse looked down at the battered piece of cardboard.\n\n“She remembered.”\n\nOf course she had.\n\nThe dead on the roster had belonged to all of them.\n\nSo had the living.\n\nDanse rubbed his thumb along the cracked edge of Keane's card. The corners had been worn soft by years of being taken down, handled, and put back on the Cambridge wall. It wasn't an official decoration. Somebody had kept it because a name on a casualty list wasn't enough.\n\n\"She could have left this there,\" he said.\n\n\"She chose to send it.\"\n\n\"Then Rhys carried it all this way.\" He sounded almost bewildered. \"After everything I believed about him, and everything he believed about me.\"\n\nI watched him turn the card over. A little of the ink had bled through the back; the printing was no longer legible.\n\n\"What will you do with it?\" I asked.\n\nHe considered the empty space on the workshop wall.\n\n\"Not put it where anyone who walks in must ask about it. Not yet.\"\n\nHe found an envelope in the desk, wrote Keane's name on the front and slid the card inside. There was nothing ceremonial about the gesture. His hands were still shaking, and he had to try twice to close the flap.\n\n\"Haylen was angry,\" he said.\n\n\"She was.\"\n\n\"Perhaps she remains angry.\"\n\n\"You can let her be.\"\n\nHe gave me a look that was almost a challenge, then shook his head. \"I intend to. I don't want a letter from her to be another order she feels compelled to obey.\"\n\nI leaned against his shoulder. He was still the same man who checked a weapon before sleep and kept lists of repairs on the pantry wall. He was also learning that some things could be preserved without being displayed, and that a relationship could survive an unfinished argument.\n\nDanse folded Haylen’s letter along the original creases.\n\n“Do you want to answer?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Tonight?”\n\n“No.”\n\nA few months ago he would have treated delay like failure.\n\nNow he set the paper beside the lamp.\n\n“Tomorrow,” he said.\n\n“Good.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“I’m learning that word.”\n\n“Tomorrow?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe future had become something he could schedule without needing permission to have it.\n\nThat night, before bed, he placed Keane’s card beneath the old holotag in the workshop.\n\nThen he looked at the words over the bench.\n\nDANSE.\n\n“Ad Victoriam,” he said softly.\n\nNot to the Brotherhood.\n\nNot away from it either.\n\nTo the life that kept becoming larger than the rules he had inherited.\n","word_count":1810}},"after-always":{"after-always-09":{"number":9,"title":"A Cup of Tea, Unpoisoned","content":"The Floo network failed while we were halfway back to Hogwarts.\n\nThat sentence makes the experience sound almost reasonable.\n\nIt was not.\n\nOne moment I was spinning through green fire with a case of Snape's journals clutched to my chest. The next, the fireplace threw me out into a pub I had never seen, directly into a basket of umbrellas.\n\nSnape arrived three seconds later with considerably more dignity and considerably less balance.\n\nI caught his elbow.\n\nHe caught the mantel.\n\nA woman behind the bar looked up from polishing a glass.\n\n“Network's down north of Birmingham,” she said. “Storm charms. Could be an hour.”\n\nSnape closed his eyes.\n\nI pulled an umbrella out from under my knee.\n\n“Well,” I said. “At least we aren't dead.”\n\nHe opened one eye.\n\n“You have set an alarmingly low standard for travel.”\n\n“It has served me well.”\n\nThe pub was called The Copper Kettle. It occupied the back half of what looked from the street like a closed tailor's shop. There were six tables, a crooked bar, and a fire that smelled of cedar.\n\nWe took the table furthest from everyone else.\n\nA small notice had been nailed to the table's edge. It listed approved Floo destinations in black ink, with three struck through in red. The innkeeper saw me studying it and shook her head.\n\n\"Don't trust that sheet. It's last week's list. Ministry says one thing; the inspector tells me another.\"\n\n\"How encouraging,\" Severus said.\n\nShe leaned an elbow on the bar. \"If you've got a school to reach, don't start sending children through until somebody puts a seal on the north grate. Yesterday a man ended up at his own funeral by mistake.\"\n\nI didn't laugh. Severus did not either. He examined the fireplace from where we sat, eyes moving from the flue to the scorch marks near the mantel.\n\n\"The wards are still active,\" he said, \"but their routing has become unreliable. Those are not the same defect.\"\n\n\"Is that supposed to comfort me?\" I asked.\n\n\"Certainly not. It is supposed to prevent you from attempting a repair with the wrong spell.\"\n\nThe woman brought a pencil and the current Ministry notice so I could copy the reference number for the castle. She had been fielding stranded travelers all day; I wondered how many had demanded that she personally fix the network.\n\n\"Would you like me to send a message to Hogwarts?\" I asked.\n\nSeverus considered. \"Yes. State where we are, what we know, and that the journals have not been lost. Do not invent a return time.\"\n\nI wrote the message, and he checked the wording without changing a single word. It was a strange intimacy, being trusted with facts rather than being reassured that every problem would soon be solved.\n\nNaturally.\n\nThe woman brought tea without asking.\n\nSnape looked at the cup.\n\nThen at her.\n\n“I remember you,” she said.\n\nHis face went blank.\n\n“You came through here in ninety-four. Bought a vial of salamander blood from my brother and told him his preservation charm was embarrassing.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“He fixed it.”\n\n“Then the humiliation was educational.”\n\nShe laughed and walked away.\n\nI stared at him.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You leave a trail of improved storage charms and emotional damage everywhere you go.”\n\n“A legacy requires consistency.”\n\nThe tea was ordinary black tea, strong enough to be useful.\n\nI added milk.\n\nSnape did not.\n\nI pointed at his cup. “Are you going to test it?”\n\nHis eyebrow lifted.\n\n“For poison,” I said.\n\n“I understood the question.”\n\n“Given your life, it seems reasonable.”\n\nHe picked up the cup and drank.\n\n“No?”\n\n“If someone wished to poison me in a provincial pub, they would deserve the success.”\n\n“That is not a healthy attitude.”\n\n“It is an efficient one.”\n\nI sighed.\n\nHe looked faintly satisfied.\n\nOutside, rain streaked the windows. The delay gave us nowhere to go and nothing useful to do. I could have read one of the journals, but that felt intrusive while he sat across from me.\n\nSo I watched the storm.\n\nSnape watched me.\n\n“You dislike idle time,” he said.\n\n“So do you.”\n\n“I asked first.”\n\n“No, you made an observation.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“Yes,” I said. “I dislike it.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nThat surprised me.\n\nHe almost never asked questions that were not tactical.\n\nI considered giving him something easy.\n\nInstead I said, “If I'm doing something, I know what I'm for.”\n\nThe words sounded too honest once they existed.\n\nSnape looked at his tea.\n\n“Utility is not identity.”\n\nI laughed once.\n\n“That's rich coming from you.”\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\n“Explain.”\n\n“You spent years making yourself useful to people who could not know what you were doing. Dumbledore needed a spy. Voldemort needed a servant. Hogwarts needed a teacher. Slytherin needed a Head of House. Harry needed someone keeping him alive from the shadows.”\n\nI stopped.\n\nSnape's face had gone very still.\n\n“I've read the public testimony,” I added. “Not your memories.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And now you're recovering by turning yourself into an evidence archive with legs.”\n\nHis lip curled.\n\n“Poetic.”\n\n“Accurate.”\n\n“Presumptuous.”\n\n“Also accurate.”\n\nThe woman behind the bar dropped a spoon. The clatter filled the pause.\n\nAt last Snape said, “And what are you when you are not useful?”\n\nThe question found a place I had not armored.\n\nI looked out the window.\n\n“Annoying, apparently.”\n\n“That is a skill, not an identity.”\n\nI smiled despite myself.\n\n“I'm still figuring it out.”\n\nHe nodded once.\n\nNo advice.\n\nNo lecture.\n\nNo attempt to fix me.\n\nI appreciated it more than reassurance.\n\nThe storm worsened.\n\nSomeone near the bar started a card game. A wireless crackled with a news report about reconstruction grants and Ministry hearings. Snape's shoulders tightened when his name was mentioned.\n\nThe announcer called him “the controversial former Headmaster.”\n\nI reached over and turned the wireless down.\n\nSnape looked at my hand.\n\n“I am capable of hearing criticism.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Then why?”\n\n“Because I was trying to drink tea, and the man's voice is irritating.”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Accurate,” Snape said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nThe woman brought sandwiches.\n\n“We didn't order these,” I said.\n\n“House rule during network outages.”\n\nSnape eyed the plate.\n\n“Unpoisoned?” I asked.\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\nI took the first bite.\n\n“Heroic,” he said.\n\n“Someone has to protect you.”\n\nHis expression changed so quickly I almost missed it.\n\nThe joke had hit something real.\n\nI put the sandwich down.\n\n“I didn't mean—”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHis voice was quiet.\n\nThat should have ended it.\n\nInstead he said, “People have protected me.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Few did so without expecting a return.”\n\n“Dumbledore?”\n\nHe looked at me sharply.\n\n“You said few. Not none.”\n\nHis gaze dropped.\n\n“Albus expected a great deal.”\n\n“That doesn't answer the question.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI waited again.\n\nHe seemed annoyed by my refusal to fill the silence.\n\nAt last he said, “He kept me alive when I gave him little reason to.”\n\n“And then used you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Both can be true.”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nIt was one of the quietest agreements we had ever reached.\n\nI picked up my sandwich.\n\nAfter a moment, he picked up his.\n\nWe ate.\n\nThe network remained down for another ninety minutes.\n\nDuring that time, I learned that Snape hated sweet tea, preferred coffee but considered most public coffee an offense, could play chess but found competitive chess players tedious, and had once brewed a hangover cure for Professor McGonagall after a staff Christmas party.\n\nHe denied the last detail as soon as he realized he had told me enough to make it funny.\n\n“You are not repeating that.”\n\n“To anyone?”\n\n“To anyone.”\n\n“Can I repeat it to you?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“What if I write it down?”\n\nHis stare promised consequences.\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe looked at me over the rim of his cup.\n\nThere was something strange in his face.\n\nNot warmth exactly.\n\nAttention.\n\nThe kind that made me suddenly aware of the shape of my hands around the teacup and the rain cooling the window beside us.\n\nMy pulse changed.\n\nSnape's eyes narrowed.\n\nOf course he noticed.\n\nHe noticed everything.\n\nI looked away first.\n\nThat was new.\n\nThe bartender came back to tell us that the network inspectors had still not cleared the northbound hearth.\n\n“People keep asking whether it's safe,” she said. “If I knew that, I'd have gone home an hour ago.”\n\nSnape set his cup down.\n\n“Then stop telling them an hour,” he said. “Tell them it is closed until inspected.”\n\nShe gave him a narrow look. “Are you volunteering to explain that to my customers?”\n\n“No. You are far more qualified to tell them they're not getting what they want.”\n\nIt sounded insulting until he passed her the official delay notice he'd copied from the Ministry board outside. There was no comforting lie in it, only the relevant route and the name of the office responsible for approving travel.\n\nShe read it and nodded. “That will help.”\n\n“Good. Because I refuse to be scattered across three counties to spare an impatient stranger five minutes.”\n\nI laughed. Severus looked at me with weary suspicion.\n\n“You find that amusing?”\n\n“Only the part where you describe surviving a transport incident as an administrative inconvenience.”\n\n“It is an administrative inconvenience,” he said. “Potentially a terminal one.”\n\nHe examined his tea with obvious disapproval but did not abandon the cup. Watching him bristle over a faulty system reminded me that he was not a soft-spoken sage in black robes. He was sharp, exacting, petty when tired, and unexpectedly generous with information that could keep somebody else safe. I found the combination more compelling than I should have.\n\n\nThe Floo flared green.\n\n“Northbound connections restored,” the bartender called.\n\nWe stood.\n\nSnape lifted the lighter case.\n\nI took the heavier one.\n\nHe did not argue.\n\nAt the hearth, he paused.\n\n“The tea was poor,” he said.\n\n“It was fine.”\n\n“It was tannic.”\n\n“So are you.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nThen the corner of his mouth lifted.\n\n“After you.”\n\nI stepped into the green fire.\n\nAs the world began to spin, I realized I had just spent two hours trapped in a pub with Severus Snape and had not once wished for the network to return sooner.\n\nThat was probably a problem.\n\nThe delay gave us time to read three of the journals properly. Two were worthless inventories. The third contained a set of notes from a St Mungo's brewer who had studied long-term nerve damage after venom exposure.\n\nSnape read the same paragraph four times.\n\n“You've found something,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You've been glaring at that page like it owes you money.”\n\nHe slid the journal across the table.\n\nThe treatment was experimental, old, and probably useless. It was also the first reference either of us had found to a tremor pattern that resembled his.\n\n“Worth asking Pomfrey?”\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“That's practically enthusiasm.”\n\nHis finger tapped the margin. “It is a twenty-seven-year-old case series with six patients.”\n\n“So: not magic.”\n\n“Despite the setting, evidence remains advisable.”\n\nI smiled. “I'll copy the citation.”\n\nHe looked at me oddly.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You did not say it would work.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Most people do.”\n\nI understood then. Hope had become another thing people tried to force on him, as if optimism were a debt he owed for surviving.\n\n“It might help,” I said. “That's enough to investigate.”\n\nHis gaze lowered to the journal.\n\n“Yes,” he said. “It is.”\n\nI carried it back to Hogwarts with the journals.","word_count":1901},"after-always-24":{"number":24,"title":"The Memory He Offers","content":"Snape gave me a memory on a Sunday afternoon.\n\nNo warning.\n\nNo explanation.\n\nHe placed a small glass vial beside my hand while I was updating the final audit index.\n\nSilver mist moved inside it.\n\nI stopped writing.\n\n“What is this?”\n\n“A memory.”\n\n“I gathered that.”\n\n“Then your question was unnecessary.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHis face was pale but composed.\n\n“Yours?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why are you giving it to me?”\n\n“Because you keep asking questions.”\n\n“I can stop.”\n\n“That would be miraculous.”\n\n“Snape.”\n\nHe looked toward the office.\n\n“There are things I am unwilling to explain twice.”\n\nThe phrasing bothered me.\n\n“Twice?”\n\n“Once to you. Again at the hearing.”\n\nI understood.\n\n“This is evidence?”\n\n“Partly.”\n\n“Then why me?”\n\nHis eyes returned to mine.\n\n“Because I would prefer one person in that room to know what it costs before it is shown.”\n\nThe hearing.\n\nHarry's testimony would include selected memories if the Wizengamot requested corroboration. Snape had fought the idea for weeks.\n\nNow he was offering me one first.\n\nI did not touch the vial.\n\n“Are you sure?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe honesty made my chest ache.\n\n“Do you still want me to watch it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Will you be there?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nOnly then did I pick it up.\n\nWe used the Pensieve in McGonagall's office because Snape refused to keep one in the dungeon.\n\nShe left us alone.\n\nThe silver surface rippled.\n\nSnape stood beside me.\n\n“Do not attempt to interact,” he said.\n\n“I know how a Pensieve works.”\n\n“Humor me.”\n\n“Rare request.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\nI touched the surface.\n\nThe world tipped.\n\nI landed in a narrow lane under a gray sky.\n\nSpinner's End.\n\nA boy stood near a hedge.\n\nThin. Black-haired. Clothes too large. Watching two girls through the leaves.\n\nI knew who he was before I saw his face.\n\nYoung Severus.\n\nThe memory did not move like a story. It moved like truth remembered: angles too sharp, sounds too clear in some places and blurred in others.\n\nLily laughed.\n\nPetunia complained.\n\nSeverus watched.\n\nI felt the adult Snape beside me but did not look at him.\n\nThe boy was lonely enough to make my throat hurt.\n\nHe was also rude.\n\nThat mattered.\n\nWhen he finally spoke to Lily, he was eager and awkward and too intense. He told her about magic like a secret he had been waiting his whole life to give someone. He insulted Petunia when she mocked him. Lily defended her sister.\n\nThe boy's face closed.\n\nEven then.\n\nWalls in miniature.\n\nThe memory shifted.\n\nHogwarts Express.\n\nA compartment.\n\nJames Potter and Sirius Black.\n\nNames I knew from history became children in front of me.\n\nCruel jokes.\n\nHouse loyalties claimed before anyone understood what they would cost.\n\nSeverus's anger came quick.\n\nSo did theirs.\n\nLily tried to keep peace.\n\nThe memory shifted again.\n\nOlder now.\n\nA corridor.\n\nLily furious.\n\nSeverus trying to apologize for a word he had called her.\n\nNot a small word.\n\nNot an accident stripped of context.\n\nA slur spoken in humiliation and anger, backed by friends who used it easily.\n\n“I didn't mean you,” the boy insisted.\n\nLily's face changed.\n\nI felt something twist inside me.\n\nBecause there it was.\n\nThe terrible logic of prejudice: not you, as if affection for one person repaired contempt for everyone like them.\n\nLily refused it.\n\nGood.\n\nI was glad she did.\n\nBeside me, adult Snape did not move.\n\nThe memory shifted.\n\nA hill.\n\nDumbledore.\n\nRain.\n\nA young man with panic in his face, begging for Lily's life after learning the prophecy he had carried was being used against her family.\n\nHe did not ask for James first.\n\nHe did not ask for Harry first.\n\nThe ugliness of that was there.\n\nSo was the desperation.\n\nDumbledore's disgust.\n\nSnape's willingness to trade anything for her safety.\n\nThen another shift.\n\nA room after her death.\n\nSnape broken in a way I had never seen him.\n\nDumbledore demanding what he would do now.\n\nThe memory ended before the answer.\n\nI rose from the Pensieve gasping.\n\nMy heel caught the edge of a carpet and I had to brace myself against the desk. Severus took a single step toward me, then stopped before he reached for my arm.\n\n\"Sit down,\" he said.\n\n\"I can stand.\"\n\n\"I can see that. The question is whether you can stand without taking my entire inventory with you.\"\n\nI sat. My hands were shaking. The silver mist continued to turn in the Pensieve, holding an hour of a childhood I had not lived and had no right to rearrange into a tidy explanation.\n\n\"Do you want me to look at any of it again?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not today.\" He drew the basin away from the edge of the table. \"The memory was given to you. It was not a license to keep examining it without asking.\"\n\nI looked up. \"What about Lily? She couldn't consent to being shown to me.\"\n\nHe held my gaze. The answer took longer than I expected.\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"And I must live with what I chose to show. I wanted you to know the harm I did as well as the things I regret losing. I should not have assumed that my need to be understood settled everyone else's privacy.\"\n\nThe admissions made the room heavier, not lighter. A minute earlier I had been inside his memory, close enough to see the mud on young Severus's shoes. Now I was an adult sitting across from a man who could not change a single word that child had said.\n\nI wrapped my fingers around the cold glass of water he'd set beside me. He didn't ask for absolution. I didn't offer any. When I could stand properly, we covered the basin and left it where he could decide what to do with it next.\n\nThe office returned.\n\nSnape stood near the window with his back to me.\n\nI gripped the edge of the table until the dizziness passed.\n\nNeither of us spoke.\n\nAt last I said, “Why that memory?”\n\n“Those memories.”\n\n“You know what I mean.”\n\nHis shoulders tightened.\n\n“Because people will tell you a cleaner version.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Potter will tell you I loved Lily. Minerva will tell you I spied for Dumbledore. The Ministry may decide those facts weigh against others.”\n\nHe turned.\n\n“I wanted you to see what the phrase loved Lily can conceal.”\n\nHis face was controlled, but his hands were clenched.\n\n“The prejudice,” I said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The slur.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The fact that your first concern was her.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Not James. Not Harry.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nEach answer cost him.\n\nI did not rescue him.\n\n“That was wrong,” I said.\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And you changed later.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Both.”\n\nA breath.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI looked back at the Pensieve.\n\n“I don't pity that boy.”\n\nSnape flinched.\n\n“I feel sorry for him,” I continued. “That's different.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“He was lonely. Poor. bullied. angry. He found people who offered belonging and fed the worst parts of him. I am sorry that happened to him.”\n\nI met Snape's gaze.\n\n“And he still hurt Lily. He still chose people who hurt others. Pain did not make those choices inevitable.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You agree.”\n\n“Of course I agree.”\n\n“Then why do you look like you're waiting for me to leave?”\n\nThe question cut through the room.\n\nSnape froze.\n\nI could see the answer before he spoke.\n\nBecause I had seen it.\n\nBecause he had handed me the ugliest version and expected disgust to complete the ritual.\n\n“I have no intention of leaving because you were an awful sixteen-year-old,” I said.\n\nHis mouth twitched despite everything.\n\n“I was awful at seventeen as well.”\n\n“Thank you for the correction.”\n\n“And twenty.”\n\n“Stop showing off.”\n\nThat got a real, brief laugh.\n\nThen his expression broke again.\n\n“You should be disgusted.”\n\n“I am disgusted by some of what I saw.”\n\nHis face went still.\n\n“I am not disgusted by your existence.”\n\nSilence.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\nNot touching.\n\n“Those are not the same thing.”\n\nSnape looked down.\n\nFor a long moment, I thought he would retreat.\n\nInstead he held out his hand.\n\nI took it.\n\nHis grip was tight.\n\n“Thank you,” I said.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For trusting me with something that did not make you look good.”\n\nHis gaze lifted.\n\n“That is a strange thing to thank someone for.”\n\n“It's a strange thing to offer.”\n\n“Perhaps.”\n\nWe stood beside the Pensieve until the silver surface became still.\n\nBefore we left, Snape took the vial back.\n\nHe held it up to the light.\n\n“I may not use it at the hearing.”\n\n“That's your choice.”\n\n“They may require corroboration.”\n\n“Then you decide what is necessary.”\n\nHis eyes moved toward me.\n\n“You are not going to tell me what is strategically best?”\n\n“Not about this.”\n\nAnother small surprise.\n\nHe pocketed the memory.\n\nAt the door, he paused.\n\n“I did mean one thing,” he said.\n\n“What?”\n\n“When I was a child. I meant it when I told her she was a witch.”\n\nI remembered the eager boy, offering magic like a door out of the gray world.\n\n“I know.”\n\nSnape nodded.\n\nI thought about the memory for days afterward.\n\nNot because I wanted more of it.\n\nBecause receiving it had changed the shape of consent between us.\n\nSeverus had spent much of his life having privacy invaded in one direction or another: Legilimency, surveillance, blackmail, school gossip, wartime secrets dragged into public judgment. Offering a memory was not the same as surrendering the right to keep others.\n\nSo when I caught myself forming a question about Lily's final year at school, I stopped.\n\nHe noticed.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That is transparently false.”\n\n“I had a question. Then I decided it wasn't mine.”\n\nHis eyes stayed on me.\n\n“You may ask.”\n\n“That's not the same as you wanting to answer.”\n\nA long pause.\n\n“No,” he said. “It is not.”\n\nHe returned to the inventory sheet.\n\nTen minutes later, without looking up, he told me a completely unrelated story about Lily once hexing a boy who had thrown mud at her sister.\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe did too, quietly.\n\nThat was how I learned the difference between information extracted and memory shared.\n\nWe walked back to the dungeon together.","word_count":1692},"after-always-39":{"number":39,"title":"What We Choose to Keep","content":"We found the Death Eater robes in a trunk beneath three generations of Prince family linens.\n\nIt was exactly the sort of absurdity Spinner's End specialized in.\n\nA hand-embroidered tablecloth. Two moth-eaten blankets. A stack of yellowed letters tied with green ribbon. Then, beneath them, folded with unbearable neatness, black fabric from a life Severus had spent years trying to outlive.\n\nHe stared into the trunk.\n\nI sat back on my heels.\n\nWe had been clearing the upstairs room for almost an hour. The laboratory was finished, his Hogwarts schedule had settled, and the house had finally reached the stage where renovation required decisions about objects rather than walls.\n\nObjects were worse.\n\nWalls did not remember you.\n\n“What do you want to do with them?” I asked.\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“I am considering whether Incendio would damage the floorboards.”\n\n“Practical.”\n\n“I strive for consistency.”\n\nHe did not reach for his wand.\n\nI did not reach for the robes.\n\nThat had become one of the most important things I knew how to do with Severus: leave space around a choice without abandoning him inside it.\n\nHe lifted the green-ribboned letters first.\n\n“My mother's.”\n\n“To whom?”\n\n“My grandmother, mostly.”\n\n“Do you want to read them?”\n\n“No.”\n\nA beat.\n\n“Not today,” he corrected.\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe had three boxes on the floor.\n\nKEEP.\n\nARCHIVE.\n\nDESTROY.\n\nThe labels had been my idea. Severus had mocked them for being simplistic, then charmed the lettering so it could not smudge.\n\nThe letters went into ARCHIVE.\n\nThe tablecloth went into KEEP after Severus admitted, with visible resentment, that his mother had embroidered it herself.\n\nA cracked silver photograph frame went into KEEP too. The picture inside showed a much younger Eileen Prince before marriage, dark-haired and unsmiling in school robes. Severus touched the edge of the image once.\n\n“My mother was not kind,” he said.\n\nI waited.\n\n“Nor was she uniformly cruel.”\n\n“People rarely cooperate with categories.”\n\n“No. Inconvenient of them.”\n\nHe set the frame carefully into the box.\n\nThe Death Eater robes remained in the trunk.\n\n“What about those?” I asked eventually.\n\nHis eyes went flat.\n\n“They are evidence.”\n\n“Of what?”\n\n“My stupidity.”\n\n“You have other evidence.”\n\nHis mouth twitched despite himself.\n\n“Abundant evidence,” I added.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“Any time.”\n\nHe looked back at the robes.\n\n“Destroying them feels theatrical.”\n\n“Keeping them could be theatrical too.”\n\nHis gaze shifted toward me.\n\n“Explain.”\n\n“People keep ugly relics because they think suffering deserves a museum. Sometimes it does. Sometimes it becomes another way to kneel in front of the past.”\n\nHe was silent.\n\nI had learned not to soften every difficult statement after saying it. Severus respected the full weight of words. He could forgive clumsiness more readily than cowardice.\n\n“I don't know which this is for you,” I said. “That's why I'm asking.”\n\nHe reached into the trunk.\n\nThe robes came free with a whisper of old fabric.\n\nFor a moment he held them against the light.\n\nThey were smaller than I expected.\n\nThat startled me more than anything.\n\nNot tiny. Not child-sized. But narrow through the shoulders, cut for a younger man who had not yet grown into the severe shape I knew. They made his recruitment suddenly physical. There had been a body inside this cloth. A young adult body. Angry, ambitious, humiliated, frightened, cruel, talented, and convinced belonging to something powerful might make the world stop making him feel small.\n\nIt did not make him innocent.\n\nIt made the mistake human-sized.\n\nSeverus saw where I was looking.\n\n“I was twenty-one when I turned spy,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I joined before that.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are doing that thing.”\n\n“What thing?”\n\n“Looking at me as though chronology is an emotion.”\n\nI exhaled through my nose. “I was thinking you were young.”\n\nHis expression hardened. “Old enough.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe hardness faltered.\n\nI did not argue him out of responsibility. He did not force me to condemn the younger version of him until there was nothing left to understand.\n\nThat was the compromise we had built over months.\n\nHe lowered the robes.\n\n“I do not want them in the house.”\n\n“Then they don't stay in the house.”\n\n“I do not want to sell them, donate them to some morbid collection, or permit a historian to turn them into a footnote about redemption.”\n\n“Then don't.”\n\nHis wand appeared in his hand.\n\nThis time he moved without hesitation.\n\nWe took the robes outside.\n\nThe narrow yard behind Spinner's End was damp from morning rain. The new laboratory windows reflected a colorless sky. Severus laid the fabric inside an old iron brazier that had once held coal.\n\nHe did not make a speech.\n\nNeither did I.\n\n“Incendio.”\n\nFire caught along the hem.\n\nThe black cloth curled inward, orange eating through old wool. A silver fastening fell loose and struck the metal basin with a sharp ping.\n\nSeverus watched until the last recognizable fold collapsed.\n\nThen he extinguished the remains.\n\n“That was less satisfying than fiction suggests,” he said.\n\n“Most symbolic acts probably are.”\n\n“Entirely inadequate.”\n\n“Want me to clap?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Cry?”\n\n“Certainly not.”\n\n“Say something about rising from ashes?”\n\n“If you do, I will put you in the brazier.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nHis shoulder touched mine.\n\nWe stood there another minute anyway.\n\nBack upstairs, the clearing became easier.\n\nWe found school essays Severus had written at fifteen, covered in red corrections from Slughorn and even denser black notes he had added later to correct the corrections. We found a broken quill, a small collection of potion bottle stoppers, three books in French, one in German, and a Muggle chemistry text so outdated that I laughed until Severus threatened to confiscate my lungs.\n\nOne of the essays had been corrected on both sides. I held it to the light and could see Severus's cramped notes pushing into the margins, arguing with a teacher decades after the original marks had been made.\n\n\"Slughorn thought your conclusion was wrong.\"\n\n\"He thought it insufficiently flattering to established theory.\"\n\n\"Was it wrong?\"\n\nSeverus took the paper and examined the formula. \"Partly. The reagent was unstable at the temperature I proposed. He was right to question it. He was not right about the mechanism.\"\n\nI looked at the corrections again. They were a conversation between a talented, unhappy child and an adult who had not always noticed the difference between brilliance and the need to be noticed.\n\n\"Would the school want these?\" I asked.\n\n\"Perhaps. As an example of how an obstinate pupil can waste ink.\"\n\n\"Or how two people can both be wrong and still produce something useful.\"\n\n\"Spare me the inscription.\"\n\nHe set the essay aside, neither in the KEEP box nor in the fire pile. We agreed to ask the archive whether it could accept a copy with both sets of notes intact. The original was his, and he would decide whether to show it. I wouldn't make his schoolwork into an exhibit about redemption without his consent.\n\nThere was an old ink stain on the edge of his thumb from the other papers. I touched it lightly. He caught my hand, brought the stained fingertip to his mouth and kissed it once, almost carelessly.\n\n\"You're going to get ink on your face,\" I said.\n\n\"Then kindly stop staring.\"\n\nI didn't. He let me stay close anyway, with the box of old papers still open between us.\n\nWe found a scarf.\n\nRed and gold.\n\nNeither of us spoke.\n\nThe fabric was faded almost to brown at the edges.\n\nHe touched it as if it might burn.\n\n“Lily's?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Mine.”\n\nI stared.\n\nHe looked murderous.\n\n“You owned a Gryffindor scarf?”\n\n“It was acquired under circumstances that are none of your concern.”\n\n“Severus.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did she give it to you?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did you steal it?”\n\nHis silence answered too much.\n\nI sat down hard on the floor and laughed.\n\n“I was eleven,” he snapped.\n\nThat made it worse.\n\nTears came to my eyes.\n\n“Stop.”\n\n“I can't.”\n\n“You are intolerable.”\n\n“You stole Lily Evans's Gryffindor scarf?”\n\n“I returned it.”\n\n“You are holding it.”\n\n“A different scarf.”\n\nI laughed harder.\n\nFor one glorious minute, Severus Snape—former spy, former Death Eater, former Headmaster, terror of generations of schoolchildren—was reduced to arguing that there had apparently been two scarves and only one had been stolen.\n\nWhen I finally recovered, he was sitting against the opposite wall with the expression of a man reconsidering every decision that had led to romance.\n\n“What box?” I asked.\n\nHe looked down at the scarf.\n\nHis thumb moved over the old wool.\n\n“Keep.”\n\nI nodded.\n\nNo jealousy arrived.\n\nNo ache of competition.\n\nJust tenderness for the ridiculous eleven-year-old who had wanted something bright enough to steal it.\n\nSeverus placed the scarf in the box beside his mother's tablecloth.\n\nThen he picked up the annotated Potions textbook.\n\n“That stays,” I said.\n\n“I had not asked.”\n\n“You were thinking too loudly.”\n\n“It is historically incriminating.”\n\n“It is also brilliant.”\n\n“Parts of it are adolescent nonsense.”\n\n“So are parts of everyone.”\n\nHe looked at the book for a long time.\n\nThen he set it in KEEP.\n\nBy evening, the room was nearly empty.\n\nThree boxes remained.\n\nKEEP was the fullest.\n\nARCHIVE held documents that mattered but did not need to live at the center of daily life.\n\nDESTROY contained surprisingly little.\n\nI thought about that as we sat on the floor eating takeaway from paper cartons because neither of us had the energy to cook.\n\nHealing, I had once imagined, meant throwing away enough of the past that the future had room.\n\nSeverus had taught me something stranger.\n\nSometimes room was made by deciding what an object was allowed to mean.\n\nA scarf could be a memory without being a shrine.\n\nA textbook could contain cruelty and genius on adjacent pages.\n\nA family photograph could survive without pretending the family had been happy.\n\nA house could remember and still change.\n\nSeverus nudged my knee with his.\n\n“You are staring.”\n\n“Thinking.”\n\n“Dangerous.”\n\n“About keeping you.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“I am not an object to be sorted into one of your boxes.”\n\n“Good. You'd exceed the weight limit.”\n\nHe took my carton away.\n\n“Hey.”\n\n“Consequences.”\n\nI leaned over and stole it back.\n\nHe let me.\n\nThat went in KEEP too.","word_count":1707}}};for(const s of lib.series){const rec=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=rec[ch.id];if(e&&ch.chapter_number===e.number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}}})();
